 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the 2023–24 peak.** Rents are not accelerating, though prices overall are rising faster than rents.\n\n**Sydney rent inflation (CPI rents, annual change)**\n\n| Period | Sydney | Australia |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mar 2024 (peak) | 8.8% | 7.7% |\n| Aug 2024 | 7.1% | 6.7% |\n| Aug 2025 (low point) | 3.2% | 3.7% |\n| Aug 2026 (latest) | 3.5% | 3.6% |\n\n- **Slowdown:** Sydney rent growth fell from 8.8% in March 2024 to 3.2% in August 2025. It has stayed between 3.2% and 3.5% since then. The latest reading is 3.5% for August 2026.\n- **Compounded increase:** Compounding the annual changes gives roughly a 14% rise in Sydney rents over the three years to August 2026 (7.1%, 3.2% and 3.5%). Including the 8.7% rise in the year to August 2023, that is about 24% over four years. These are my own calculations from the annual figures. Rents don't fall back after growth slows, so the higher level stays.\n- **Against overall inflation:** Sydney's all-groups CPI was up 3.9% in the year to August 2026, so rent growth of 3.5% is now slightly below general inflation. That is a change from 2023–24, when rents were growing faster than overall prices. Australia-wide CPI was 4.0% in the same month.\n\n**Caveats**\n- CPI rents measure what all tenants pay, including those on existing leases. They move more slowly than advertised rents for new leases, so a new renter in Sydney may face bigger increases than 3.5%.\n- I haven't looked at vacancy rates, asking rents or rent relative to income, so this doesn't show how affordable rent is. I can check wages, unemployment or the cash rate if you'd like more on that.",
